lenses.menu_architecture:
Narrow, speed-engineered menu: about 22 SKUs, of which ≤8 are food (ASSUMPTION). Basis: customisation causes 43% of US order errors (C6 [17]), and the window target is ≤60 s (C6 §3.5).

(1) Core beverages, about 70% of sales. Ten espresso SKUs and modifiers:
- americano, cappuccino, latte, flat white, raf;
- hot in 350 or 470 ml, iced in 450 ml;
- 'Armenian soorj to go', 250 ml, as the local entry SKU (A2 §4.3 suggests testing it);
- three signature sweet iced drinks, including an apricot one tied to the identity (A2: growth is in sweet, milky and iced drinks);
- tea, hot chocolate and a kids' drink.
Energy drinks stay ≤5% (C6: the US share of ~25% does not transfer).

(2) Food, about 27–31% of sales, all bake-off or pre-assembled and finished in ≤60–90 s, with no fryers, no grills and an all-electric kitchen (C5: avoid the LPG/gas permit path):
- plain croissant;
- two sweet pastries (gata-style, muffin);
- two hot hand-held breakfasts (egg-and-cheese croissant; lavash breakfast wrap or khachapuri-style hand pie);
- one premium sandwich or wrap.
Supply comes from a licensed Yerevan bakery or frozen dough until there are 3 units, then from a central prep kitchen (ASSUMPTION). Confirm with the FSIB whether bake-off staff count as 'pastry chefs' under Art. 12(6).

(3) Bundles:
- Commuter combo, latte 350 + hot hand-held: 2,700 (−7% vs à la carte);
- Latte + sandwich: 3,500, within A2's 3,500–3,900 combo band;
- an optional weekend 'road box' for leisure cars.

(4) Packaged water and juice only, under 5% of sales, labelled in Armenian at ≥3 mm type (C5 [10]). No convenience-store range.

(5) Channels: two order lanes with tablet runners, two pickup windows, a walk-up window, and 12 seats plus a terrace. Only 42% of Yerevan households own a car (A5 [8]), and Black Rock has lobbies at 73% of stores (C6 [5]). Order-ahead runs through the app from launch, with loyalty targeted at 10–30% of transactions by year 3 (C6 ASSUMPTION). No delivery-platform listing at launch.

Menu boards are Armenian-first and show ml or grams and the final price (Law on Trade and Services Art. 12(3); Law on Advertising Art. 5; C5). The 'Ճամփադ բաց!' send-off at the window is the hospitality ritual. Intouch 2026 found that friendly visits were also 25 s faster (C6).

problems.fix:
Replace "build at ≤AMD 110–150M" with a stage-gated plan.

Phase 0 (about 6 months, small budget):
- summer and winter counts at the Coffee House M4 drive-thru;
- intercept surveys and receipt sampling at Coffee House and Max Cafe km 12.2;
- a Van Westendorp price survey, n ≥ 600;
- a 2-week fuel-station pop-up.
These are already specified in A2 §5, A4 §5 and C6 §5.

Unit 1 is a relocatable test kiosk with a capex ceiling of about AMD 35M (ESTIMATE: 4 years × lens 1's 8.7M EBITDA at 150 tx/day). The absolute ceiling is about AMD 80M (ESTIMATE: C6 §3.9 formula re-run at ATV 1,850 = 150 × 1,850 × 360 × 15–20% × 2.5–4 years = AMD 37–80M).

Commit to a permanent mid unit only after 6–12 months at 190–220 tx/day or more.

State explicitly that the prototype needs about 387 tx/day for a 5-year payback (lens 1).

Drop "American-style" from consumer positioning and from the headline of the investor pitch.

Keep it internally as measurable standards (C6, Intouch 2026):
- window time of 60 s or less;
- order-to-handoff in 3 min or less;
- accuracy of 94–96% or more, the Dutch Bros / Starbucks level.

Lead with "fast, hot, local" under the CHAMPA brand. Do not market CHAMPA as a Starbucks alternative.

Send RFQs before freezing the price board:
- coffee: AfroLab, FEIN, Lumen, Nero Bean, Caffeine and Royal Armenia;
- milk: Marianna and Dili;
- syrups: Alpha Food Service;
- frozen viennoiserie: Russian producers, quoted delivered to Yerevan (DAP).

Show break-even at 16,000 AMD/kg and 850 AMD/L as a standard downside case.
